From 3f94bd42ce859e9071782478fc4a416595eaa789 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: terminills <tdeters@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 19:06:31 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 610/638] Revert "Honour PageAccessibilityDisposition in the
 AROS decommit path"

This reverts 38906124a6. The change is right on paper and wrong in practice:
it kills the browser on a real page.

Measured, same site (https://aros.sourceforge.io/), same runner, same 8192 MB:

  chrome 2026-09-08 (control)      renders fully              0 traps
  chrome + 38906124a6             "Loading..." forever        1 trap
  chrome - 38906124a6 (this)       renders fully              0 traps

The failing build and the passing one differ by ELEVEN ninja edges - this
header and the link - so the attribution is clean.

  Task : CrBrowserMain
  Function Exec_119_FreePooled +0x3a
  TRAPNO=0x0d (#GP)  CR2=0  ERR=0
  RAX = RDI = 0x458948d02948ca89

0x458948d02948ca89 is x86 instruction bytes (89 ca / 48 29 d0 / 48 89 45...),
i.e. code used as a pointer and handed to FreePooled. #GP with CR2=0 and code
in a register is a non-canonical address, not a page fault.

Why the "correct" change makes things worse: decommit previously stripped every
range to inaccessible, so a stale access faulted immediately, at the site of
the mistake. Honouring kAllowKeepForPerf leaves 122 ranges per page load
readable and zeroed - which is what POSIX MADV_DONTNEED gives you, and what
Linux Chromium relies on - so a stale read now silently returns zeroes and the
corruption propagates until something dereferences a garbage pointer far from
the cause. The commit did not introduce the bug; it removed the guard rail that
was containing it.

So this revert is a deliberate band-aid, and the underlying use-after-free is
the real defect. Re-land this only after that is fixed - and note the revert
restores a strictness AROS does not need, at the cost of 122 unnecessary
protection strips per page load.

Evidence: build-logs/evidence/chrome-{control-sep08,pafix-verify,revert-confirm}-20260912
---
 .../page_allocator_internals_aros.h           | 25 +++----------------
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 22 deletions(-)

diff --git a/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_aros.h b/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_aros.h
index c43aea1aa4..50eb6ceafb 100644
--- a/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_aros.h
+++ b/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_aros.h
@@ -553,33 +553,14 @@ uintptr_t TrimMappingInternal(uintptr_t base_address,
 void DecommitSystemPagesInternal(
     uintptr_t address,
     size_t length,
-    PageAccessibilityDisposition accessibility_disposition) {
+    PageAccessibilityDisposition) {
   APTR KernelBase = OpenKernelResource();
   PA_CHECK(KernelBase);
-  // Honour the disposition, as POSIX does. page_allocator_internals_posix.h
-  // computes the same flag and then makes pages inaccessible "unless the
-  // caller requested to keep permissions", and the enum itself documents
-  // kAllowKeepForPerf as "will not update permissions, if the platform
-  // supports that (POSIX & Fuchsia only)".
-  //
-  // This function previously ignored the parameter and always stripped access
-  // to zero, which is STRICTER than the platform it emulates: a caller that
-  // decommits kAllowKeepForPerf and then reads the range is fine on Linux,
-  // where the pages stay mapped, and takes a page fault here.
-  const bool change_permissions =
-      accessibility_disposition == PageAccessibilityDisposition::kRequireUpdate;
-  TracePageEvent(change_permissions ? "decommit" : "decommit-keep", address,
-                 length, 0);
+  TracePageEvent("decommit", address, length, 0);
   SetProtection(KernelBase, address, length,
                 static_cast<KRN_MapAttr>(MAP_Readable | MAP_Writable));
-  // DiscardSystemPagesInternal() is a no-op on AROS, so this zeroing is what
-  // emulates the discard. Keeping it on both paths means a reader of a
-  // kAllowKeepForPerf range sees zeroes, which is what a POSIX caller gets
-  // back from MADV_DONTNEED.
   std::memset(reinterpret_cast<void*>(address), 0, length);
-  if (change_permissions) {
-    SetProtection(KernelBase, address, length, static_cast<KRN_MapAttr>(0));
-  }
+  SetProtection(KernelBase, address, length, static_cast<KRN_MapAttr>(0));
 }
 
 bool DecommitAndZeroSystemPagesInternal(uintptr_t address,
